[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63760,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastasizing leiomyoma is an extrauterine (most commonly in the lung), well-demarcated, often nodular proliferation of benign-appearing smooth muscle, often in patients with a history of uterine leiomyoma(s).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63761,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8898/1 Metastasizing leiomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63762,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E86.0 &amp; XH1EX8 Leiomyoma of uterus &amp; Metastasizing leiomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63763,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63764,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63765,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lung (most common), abdominopelvic and/or mediastinal lymph nodes</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63766,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions are incidentally discovered on radiological studies performed for other indications, but patients may present with respiratory symptoms (e.g. dyspnoea, cough) {27514603; 11373202}. Pulmonary lesions can be multiple and bilateral. They occur in patients with a history of prior hysterectomy or myomectomy, ranging from months to decades earlier {26321441}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63767,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Benign metastasizing leiomyoma is rare. The tumour typically affects women of reproductive age.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63768,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These lesions are considered to represent spread from a histologically benign uterine smooth muscle tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63769,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It has been shown that benign metastasizing leiomyoma is clonally derived from uterine leiomyoma {16357844}. Molecular analysis reveals broad similarities with the mutations and expression abnormalities found in leiomyoma, including <em>MED12</em> mutations {29484624; 18382364}. However, cytogenetic analysis highlighted chromosomal aberrations not typically found in uterine leiomyomas, including 19q and 22q terminal deletion {17460458}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63770,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastasizing leiomyomas are well-circumscribed, white to tan nodules with a bulging cut surface, usually 2&ndash;5&nbsp;mm in size.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63771,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is usually a well-demarcated proliferation of intersecting fascicles of spindle cells with moderate eosinophilic cytoplasm and blunt-ended nuclei; the cells are cytologically bland, with minimal to absent mitoses. Metastasizing leiomyoma may be cellular or admixed with adipose tissue {12649679}. In the lung, it tends to have a peribronchiolar pattern and may entrap alveolar spaces peripherally {17460458}. ER and PR are positive {10506455}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63772,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63773,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63774,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> smooth muscle tumour without atypia, or necrosis and minimal to absent mitosis, in lungs or lymph nodes in a patient with a history of myomectomy or hysterectomy for leiomyoma(s); no history of gynaecological or non-gynaecological leiomyosarcoma or intravascular leiomyomatosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63775,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastasizing leiomyoma is not usually staged.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63776,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 240,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cases have an indolent course, but extensive disease may lead to respiratory failure and death {11037349; 26321441}. Lesions are hormonally sensitive; they may respond to progestin, aromatase inhibitors, and/or GnRH agonists {15240591; 16343217}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:02:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]